uddyMoE: Exploiting Expert Redundancy to Accelerate Memory-Constrained Mixture-of-Experts Inference ... DOI:10.48550/arXiv.2511.10054 ... Image: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nPreprint\n\n# BuddyMoE: Exploiting Expert Redundancy to Accelerate Memory-Constrained Mixture-of-Experts Inference\n\n  * November 2025\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2511.10054\n\nAuthors:\n\nYun Wang\n\n  * Shanghai Jiao Tong University\n\nLingyun Yang\n\nLingyun Yang\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nSenhao Yu\n\nSenhao Yu\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nYixiao Wang\n\nYixiao Wang\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\n\n## Abstract\n\nMixture-of-Experts (MoE) architectures scale language models by activating only a subset of specialized expert networks for each input token, thereby reducing the number of floating-point operations. However, the growing size of modern MoE models causes their full parameter sets to exceed GPU memory capacity; for example, Mixtral-8x7B has 45 billion parameters and requires 87 GB of memory even though only 14 billion parameters are used per token. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBuddyMoE: Exploiting Expert Redundancy to Accelerate Memory-Constrained Mixture-of-Experts Inference | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2511.10054v1)\nciteturn2344search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Crawled: last month; This paper presents a key system for offloading model parameters from GPU to CPU memory, which is the fundamental problem scenario that necessitates BuddyMoE. ... @misc{wang2025buddymoeexploitingexpertredundancy, title={BuddyMoE: Exploiting Expert Redundancy to Accelerate Memory-Constrained Mixture-of-Experts Inference}, author={Yun Wang and Lingyun Yang and Senhao Yu and Yixiao Wang and Ruixing Li and Zhixiang Wei and James Yen and Zhengwei Qi}, year={2025}, eprint={2511.10054}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.LG}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.10054}, }\n## The Memory Constraint Challenge\n\nCurrent solutions employ parameter offloading, moving inactive experts to slower storage like CPU memory or SSDs. Systems like DeepSpeed's ZeRO-Infinity and Hugging Face Accelerate use this approach, but it introduces severe performance penalties due to data transfer latency. Loading an expert from CPU memory over PCIe can take tens of milliseconds—orders of magnitude longer than the actual computation time.\n\nTo mitigate this bottleneck, predictive prefetching systems like MoE-Infinity attempt to anticipate which experts will be needed and load them proactively. However, expert selection is highly context-dependent and difficult to predict perfectly. When prefetching fails, systems must either:\n\n  * Incur substantial PCIe latency for on-demand loading (degrading speed)\n  * Skip the expert entirely (significantly degrading accuracy)\n\n## BuddyMoE's Core Innovation\n\nBuddyMoE introduces a fundamentally different approach: instead of trying to eliminate prefetch misses through better prediction, it aims to tolerate them gracefully by exploiting inherent expert redundancy. The key insight is that many experts in large MoE models perform functionally similar operations, allowing for intelligent substitution when a needed expert is unavailable.\n\n## Methodology\n\n### Offline Profiling Phase\n\nBuddyMoE first identifies \"buddy\" experts through offline analysis:\n\n  1. Data Collection: Over a representative profiling corpus, the system records:\n\n     * Per-expert activations: how often each expert is selected\n     * Pairwise co-activations: how often expert pairs are simultaneously selected\n  2. Co-activation Analysis: This data forms expert co-activation matrices that reveal functional relationships and redundancy patterns.\n\n  3. Buddy List Construction: For each expert i, buddies are selected using a Cumulative Frequency Threshold (CFT). The conditional co-activation distribution is calculated as:\n\n$$q_{j|i} = \\frac{M_\\ell(i,j)}{\\sum_{j'} M_\\ell(i,j')}$$q j∣i​=∑j′​M ℓ​(i,j′)M ℓ​(i,j)​\n\nExperts are ranked by their $q_{j|i}$q j∣i​ values, and the buddy list includes the top peers until their cumulative co-activation frequency reaches threshold $\\alpha$α.\n\n### Runtime Replacement Mechanism\n\nDuring inference, BuddyMoE employs a three-stage decision process:\n\n  1. Token Activating Entropy (TAE) Gate: Measures a token's sensitivity to expert substitution:\n\n$$\\text{TAE}_\\ell(x) = -\\sum_{i \\in S_\\ell(x)} \\tilde{p}_\\ell(i|x) \\log \\tilde{p}_\\ell(i|x) / \\log k$$TAE ℓ​(x)=−i∈S ℓ​(x)∑​p~​ℓ​(i∣x)lo g p~​ℓ​(i∣x)/lo g k\n\nIf TAE is below threshold $τ$τ, replacement is forbidden to preserve accuracy for sensitive tokens.\n\n  2. Expert Distribution Gate: Prevents risky replacements when too many requested experts are CPU-resident, calculated as the fraction $δ_\\ell(B)$δ ℓ​(B) of CPU-resident experts in the current batch.\n\n  3. Buddy Selection: When replacement is permitted, the system selects the best GPU-resident buddy using a priority score that combines:\n\n     * Global similarity (co-activation frequency)\n     * Local compatibility (router logit scores)\n     * Topology penalties for distributed setups\n\nImage: BuddyMoE workflow diagram\n\n## Experimental Results\n\nBuddyMoE was evaluated on DeepSeek-V2-Lite using ARC benchmarks across different cache rates (fraction of experts in GPU memory):\n\n### High Memory Constraint (Cache Rate = 0.75):\n\n  * Original Baseline: 0.735 accuracy, 34.23 tokens/second\n  * BuddyMoE: 0.695 accuracy (-5.4%), 36.75 tokens/second (+7.4%)\n  * Random Replacement: 0.55 accuracy (-25.2%), 39.67 tokens/second\n\n### Moderate Memory Constraint (Cache Rate = 0.50):\n\n  * Original Baseline: 28.56 tokens/second\n  * BuddyMoE: 0.635 accuracy, 30.21 tokens/second (+5.8%)\n  * Random Replacement: 0.23 accuracy (unusable), 33.14 tokens/second\n\n### Extreme Memory Constraint (Cache Rate = 0.375):\n\n  * Original Baseline: 24.78 tokens/second\n  * BuddyMoE: 0.645 accuracy, 27.33 tokens/second (+10.3%)\n  * Random Replacement: 0.16 accuracy (unusable)\n\nImage: Performance comparison across cache rates\n\nBuddyMoE also demonstrated 20% lower PCIe bandwidth usage compared to baseline methods, indicating more efficient data transfer patterns.\n\n## Expert Redundancy Analysis\n\nThe paper's analysis reveals structured patterns in expert behavior:\n\nImage: Expert activation distribution\n\nExpert activation follows a heavy-tailed distribution, with some experts being much more frequently used than others.\n\nImage: Expert co-activation heatmap\n\nThe co-activation heatmap shows sparse but intense patterns, confirming that certain expert pairs have strong functional relationships—the foundation for BuddyMoE's substitution strategy.\n\n## Significance and Impact\n\nBuddyMoE represents a paradigm shift from perfect prediction to graceful degradation in MoE inference systems. Its key contributions include:\n\n  1. Practical Deployment: Enables large MoE models to run efficiently on memory-constrained hardware\n  2. System Resilience: Maintains performance even when prefetching fails\n  3. Efficiency Gains: Consistent 7-10% throughput improvements with minimal accuracy loss\n  4. Broad Applicability: Integrates transparently into existing MoE serving infrastructures\n\nThe approach opens new research directions in co-design of model architectures and inference systems, where model properties like redundancy become resources for system optimization rather than inefficiencies to eliminate.\n\nOutrageously Large Neural Networks: The Sparsely-Gated Mixture-of-Experts Layer\n\nThis is the seminal paper that introduced the sparsely-gated Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architecture. Since BuddyMoE is a system designed specifically to optimize inference for this type of model, this citation is foundational for understanding the core concepts and context of the main paper.\n\n[16] Noam Shazeer, *Azalia Mirhoseini, *Krzysztof Maziarz, Andy Davis, Quoc Le, Geoffrey Hinton, and Jeff Dean. 2017. Outrageously Large Neural Networks: The Sparsely-Gated Mixture-of-Experts Layer. In International Conference on Learning Representations. https://openreview.net/forum?id=B1ckMDqlg\n\nZeRO-Infinity: Breaking the GPU Memory Wall for Extreme Scale Deep Learning\n\nThis paper presents a key system for offloading model parameters from GPU to CPU memory, which is the fundamental problem scenario that necessitates BuddyMoE. The main paper identifies systems like ZeRO-Infinity as pioneers in enabling large model deployment but also highlights the I/O bottleneck they create, which BuddyMoE aims to solve.\n\n[15] Samyam Rajbhandari, Olatunji Ruwase, Jeff Rasley, Shaden Smith, and Yuxiong He. 2021. ZeRO-Infinity: Breaking the GPU Memory Wall for Extreme Scale Deep Learning. arXiv:2104.07857 [cs.DC] https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.07857\n\nPre-gated MoE: An Algorithm-System Co-Design for Fast and Scalable Mixture-of-Expert Inference\n\n@misc{wang2025buddymoeexploitingexpertredundancy, title={BuddyMoE: Exploiting Expert Redundancy to Accelerate Memory-Constrained Mixture-of-Experts Inference}, author={Yun Wang and Lingyun Yang and Senhao Yu and Yixiao Wang and Ruixing Li and Zhixiang Wei and James Yen and Zhengwei Qi}, year={2025}, eprint={2511.10054}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.LG}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.10054}, }\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Yun Wang 0039 (https://dblp.org/pid/36/3235-39)\nciteturn2344search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: 5 months ago;       * journals/corr/abs-2511-10054 ... BuddyMoE: Exploiting Expert Redundancy to Accelerate Memory-Constrained Mixture-of-Experts Inference.\n\nYun Wang, Lingyun Yang, Senhao Yu, Yixiao Wang, Ruixing Li, Zhixiang Wei, James Yen, Zhengwei Qi:\nBuddyMoE: Exp